<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/specifications/variance-script-narration> {
  ex:scene-chemometrics-variance-dispersion-title cd:narration "A mean describes a center, but not the variation around that center. This lecture develops measures of spread from observed residuals, explains why estimating the mean changes variance estimation, and connects variation of sample means to random uncertainty. The practical interpretation always depends on what was measured and which sampling assumptions are justified."@en .
  ex:scene-vr02 cd:narration "The measurement series 9, 10 and 13 mg/L and 5, 10 and 17 mg/L have the same arithmetic mean, $32/3$ mg/L. The second series is much more dispersed.\n\nA report containing only their common mean would conceal this difference. We therefore need a quantity that describes the distances between the observations and their center."@en .
  ex:scene-vr03 cd:narration "Spread matters when judging agreement among repeated laboratory measurements. A tightly clustered series and a widely scattered series can have similar means while supporting different statements about repeatability. Such comparisons require consistent units and stated measurement conditions. An illustrative target interval helps discuss results, but it is not automatically a validated analytical acceptance requirement."@en .
  ex:scene-vr04 cd:narration "Return to the same two three-observation series. Their common mean provides one fixed reference within each observed sample. The plotted stems show signed distances from each observation to that fitted mean. Series A is more tightly clustered than Series B. No population mean or population variance has been measured merely by computing this shared sample center."@en .
  ex:scene-vr05 cd:narration "A residual is an observed value minus the mean of the same observations. Residuals retain the measurement unit and indicate both direction and distance: negative values fall below the fitted mean, and positive values above it. We can calculate all these quantities from the sample, without knowing the population mean. Signed residuals alone do not provide a useful total measure of spread because they cancel."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-zero cd:narration "The signed residuals sum exactly to zero when the center is the arithmetic mean of those same observations. This is an algebraic identity, not evidence that the measurements have no variation. The complete optional derivation separates the finite sum of observations from the repeated fitted mean. Squaring the residuals will avoid cancellation while retaining the size of their deviations."@en .
  ex:scene-vr07 cd:narration "Squaring each residual makes every contribution nonnegative, so opposite signs no longer cancel. The sum of squared residuals increases when observations lie farther from their fitted center.\n\nIts unit is the square of the measurement unit, here $(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$. Because the total also depends on the number of contributions, we next normalize it by the sample size."@en .
  ex:scene-vr08 cd:narration "The mean squared deviation, MSD, is the sum of squared residuals divided by the number of observations. It is a descriptive summary of this particular sample around its own fitted mean. Series A and Series B have the same center, but different MSD values. Describing the observed sample and estimating an unknown population variance are related tasks, not identical definitions."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-compare cd:narration "The empirical MSD is calculated from lowercase observed values and their sample mean. In contrast, the theoretical variance is the expected squared distance of a random measurement $X$ from its expectation $\\mu$.\n\nThe expectation is a probability-weighted theoretical average, not a guarantee about one finite dataset. The generally unknown population variance $\\sigma^2$ is the quantity we would like to estimate from observations."@en .
  ex:scene-bf-e-rules cd:narration "Expectation leaves a fixed constant unchanged, responds linearly to fixed scaling and shifting, and distributes over a fixed finite sum. The required expectations must exist; independence is not needed for this finite linearity.\n\nA useful long-run interpretation comes from the law of large numbers: for independent identically distributed observations with a finite expected absolute value, their sample average converges to the population expectation. A finite observed average can still differ from it."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-rules cd:narration "A fixed constant has zero variance. Adding a fixed shift does not change spread, whereas scaling a random quantity by $a$ multiplies its variance by $a^2$.\n\nFor a sum of two random quantities, the general identity includes twice their covariance. Covariance is the expected product of their centered deviations. With finite second moments, independence implies zero covariance, but zero covariance does not generally imply independence."@en .
  ex:scene-lake cd:narration "The lake scenario separates a model from one realized sample. Under the illustrative model, the population mean and variance are specified; in a real application these parameters would generally be unknown. The five listed observations were generated using the preserved seed and can be used to calculate a sample mean and MSD. Neither calculation determines the actual error of this one mean relative to an unknown population center."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-question cd:narration "We now ask whether the random MSD statistic is unbiased for the population variance. Uppercase observations and their fitted mean vary across hypothetical repeated samples. Unbiasedness compares the expectation of this statistic with $\\sigma^2$; it does not assert equality for each realization.\n\nA single MSD may lie above or below the true variance. Simulated averages approximate an expectation but do not by themselves establish a mathematical identity."@en .
  ex:scene-vb-mc cd:narration "Repeated sampling makes the distinction between one estimate and its long-run behavior visible. The simulation draws independent samples of sizes 2 through 100 from the illustrative normal population with mean 10 mg/L and variance $5\\,(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$.\n\nIndividual MSD estimates fluctuate. Larger samples change that fluctuation, but the location of one simulated point does not establish or refute unbiasedness."@en .
  ex:scene-vs-mc cd:narration "For each selected sample size, the existing Monte Carlo experiment averages MSD estimates over 5,000 independent samples. These averages indicate a systematic shortfall relative to the known model variance. The simulation is numerical evidence with finite repetition error. We retain it as an illustration and next use an exact identity and expectation rules to explain the shortfall analytically."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-algebra cd:narration "An observation's distance from its fitted sample mean equals its distance from the population mean minus the shift between the two centers. Squaring, summing and collecting terms gives the central normalized identity: the MSD equals the average population-centered squared distance minus the squared mean shift.\n\nThe removed contribution is nonnegative for every realization. The optional full algebra identifies each expansion and summation rule; the identity alone is not yet an expectation proof."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-expect cd:narration "Apply expectation to the central identity. Finite linearity distributes expectation over the averaged squared distances and the subtracted squared shift. Each population-centered squared observation has expectation $\\sigma^2$ under common finite observation moments.\n\nBecause the sample mean has expectation $\\mu$, the expected squared shift of that mean is its variance. This yields $\\operatorname{E}[\\mathrm{MSD}_n]=\\sigma^2-\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)$. No numerical value for the unknown population mean was inserted from a real sample."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-helper cd:narration "The needed helper result is $\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\sigma^2/n$. Common expectations, common finite variances and pairwise zero covariance suffice; iid finite-variance observations meet these conditions, and normality is unnecessary.\n\nThe complete optional proof starts from the definition of the sample mean, establishes its expectation, then uses variance scaling and the vanishing covariance terms. This variance describes hypothetical variation of the mean estimator, not the known squared error of one observed mean."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-bias cd:narration "Substitute the sample-mean variance into the expected MSD identity. Subtracting $\\sigma^2/n$ from $\\sigma^2$ leaves $(n-1)\\sigma^2/n$.\n\nThus the MSD underestimates the population variance in expectation by the factor $(n-1)/n$ under the stated sampling assumptions. This is an average-bias statement, not a promise that every observed MSD will be smaller than the population variance."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-correction cd:narration "For $n>1$, multiply the MSD statistic by the reciprocal bias factor $n/(n-1)$. Linearity of expectation then gives a corrected statistic with expectation $\\sigma^2$.\n\nThe correction compensates for estimating the center from the same observations. It does not remove the random variation between individual samples, and taking the square root of an unbiased variance estimator does not automatically give an unbiased standard-deviation estimator."@en .
  ex:scene-cv-practical cd:narration "In practice, calculate the corrected sample variance by dividing the observed sum of squared residuals by $n-1$. The uppercase statistic $S^2$ varies across hypothetical samples; lowercase $s^2$ denotes the value calculated from the observed data.\n\nThis practical formula requires the observations, their fitted mean and a sample size greater than one, but no knowledge of $\\mu$ or $\\sigma^2$. Its unbiasedness remains conditional on the sampling assumptions."@en .
  ex:scene-vm-recap cd:narration "The expectation calculation explains the Monte Carlo shortfall. The chart compares the original simulated MSD averages, their paired corrected averages, and the theoretical expected MSD curve. Agreement of the simulation with theory is a useful numerical check. It remains important to distinguish finite simulated averages from exact theoretical expectations and to retain the same underlying sampling model when comparing them."@en .
  ex:scene-vr19 cd:narration "Apply Bessel's correction to Series A and B. Both means are $32/3$ mg/L. Their squared-residual totals are $26/3$ and $218/3$ $(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$, respectively.\n\nWith three observations, the corrected divisor is two, giving sample variances $13/3$ and $109/3$ $(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$. The calculation illustrates the same estimator for differently dispersed samples without requiring either unknown population variance."@en .
  ex:scene-vr21 cd:narration "Standard deviation is the square root of variance. The result returns to the original measurement unit and is therefore easier to compare with measured concentrations. A larger sample SD indicates greater observed spread under the stated conditions. The square root is a separate nonlinear transformation: unbiasedness of sample variance is not transferred automatically to the sample SD."@en .
  ex:scene-vr22 cd:narration "Read the sample SD formula as a sequence of operations: center each observation at the sample mean, square the residuals, sum them, apply the corrected divisor $n-1$, and take the square root. The sample size must exceed one.\n\nThis describes sample spread in the original unit; it does not by itself state uncertainty of a mean or establish agreement with a reference value."@en .
  ex:scene-vr23 cd:narration "Returning to the two original series confirms why a mean alone is insufficient. Their centers agree, but the corrected variances and SDs differ substantially because Series B is more dispersed.\n\nThe SD is expressed in mg/L, whereas variance is in $(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$. These are summaries of the same observed samples, not new measurement data or a direct determination of population parameters."@en .
  ex:scene-vr24 cd:narration "The first R exercise introduces vectors, assignment and arithmetic means. The function `c()` collects measurements, the assignment operator stores them under a name, and `mean()` calculates their sample average.\n\nRunning the program in the presentation returns the mean for A followed by B, in mg/L. Before changing 13 to 14, predict which mean will change, then run again and compare the result with that prediction."@en .
  ex:scene-vr25 cd:narration "The second R exercise calculates variance and SD for the same two series. R's `var()` uses the corrected $n-1$ divisor, and `sd()` takes the square root of that sample variance.\n\nThe output order is variance A, variance B, SD A and SD B. Compare these values with the manual worked example, remembering that variances and SDs have different units. The accompanying function legend explains the program, which can be run in the presentation."@en .
  ex:scene-vr26 cd:narration "Observed SD can summarize agreement among repeated measurements under defined conditions and is therefore relevant to precision. It does not establish trueness: a narrow cluster can be systematically displaced from a reference. Interpret the QC example with its measurement conditions and units in mind. A small observed spread from a short series is not proof of reliable long-term performance."@en .
  ex:scene-vr27 cd:narration "Precision statements need specified conditions. Repeatability concerns a short interval with relevant conditions held constant; intermediate precision includes stated changes within one laboratory; reproducibility considers specified comparisons between laboratories. These terms describe experimental conditions, not a universal numerical ranking. Report which operations, instruments, operators, days or laboratories were actually varied when obtaining the observations."@en .
  ex:scene-vb-quality cd:narration "Precision, trueness and bias address different questions. Precision concerns agreement among repeated results, while trueness concerns the relation between a long-run average and a suitable reference. Bias is the difference between an expected result and that reference. An observed mean difference estimates bias with uncertainty. A measurement series can be tightly clustered yet systematically shifted, so precision cannot substitute for an assessment of trueness."@en .
  ex:scene-vr29 cd:narration "The two illustrated series have equal observed SDs but different means, 10 and 11 mg/L, relative to a reference of 10 mg/L. Equal spread therefore does not imply equal agreement of their centers with the reference. The comparison keeps the concentration scale unchanged so that the shift remains visible. Precision alone cannot decide which average is better aligned with a reference or establish trueness from finite data."@en .
  ex:scene-vr30 cd:narration "The independent experimental unit depends on what is repeated. Three injections of one vial repeat instrumental operations, but share the preparation and field sample. Separate preparations add preparation variation; independently collected field samples can also represent collection variation. Shared conditions may still create correlation. Treating nested technical readings as independent field evidence exaggerates the effective information in the experiment."@en .
  ex:scene-vr31 cd:narration "An analytical result can be influenced by sampling, preparation, injection, detection and other sources of variation. The process diagram identifies where contributions may arise, but a list of sources does not quantify their variances. The replication design must deliberately repeat the operations whose variation is to be assessed. Shared upstream operations remain shared even when downstream readings are numerous."@en .
  ex:scene-uq-components cd:narration "Variance contributions can be combined on a common squared-unit scale when the model and covariance assumptions justify it. For pairwise uncorrelated additive contributions, the total variance is the sum of their variances. If contributions are correlated, covariance terms remain. The formula is conditional on the chosen model of the analytical process, rather than an automatic rule for every collection of SD values."@en .
  ex:scene-uq-component-sd cd:narration "After combining variance contributions, take one square root to obtain the total SD in the original unit. This gives a root-sum-of-squares relation under the stated zero-covariance assumptions, not the sum of the individual SDs. The operation makes sense only when contributions refer to compatible quantities and scales. Correlation or an inappropriate process model requires a different calculation."@en .
  ex:scene-uq-matrix cd:narration "The replication matrix connects each source of variation to the operation that must be repeated to investigate it. Repeated injections cannot by themselves estimate variation between independent field samples. Separate preparations and independently collected units answer broader questions, provided their errors are appropriately independent. State both the nesting and the experimental unit when interpreting the resulting spread."@en .
  ex:scene-vr32 cd:narration "The preserved lake observations illustrate variation between sites as well as repeated observations within a site. Site summaries and within-site SDs refer to different levels of the design. Their interpretation depends on what was independently sampled and what conditions were shared. Differences between site means cannot be reduced to instrumental repeatability, and multiple readings from one site are not automatically independent samples of the whole lake."@en .
  ex:scene-vr33 cd:narration "Relative standard deviation expresses sample SD relative to the magnitude of the sample mean, conventionally as a percentage. The numerator and denominator must use the same unit on a meaningful ratio scale. RSD can help compare relative spread across suitable concentration levels, but it does not provide an assessment of trueness or a universal analytical acceptance threshold."@en .
  ex:scene-vr34 cd:narration "The QC comparison puts both methods on compatible units before calculating relative spread. Method B's stated unit conversion changes its mean and SD by the same factor, so the RSD is unchanged. Compare the observed RSDs in the context of the specified measurement conditions. A relative-precision comparison does not by itself establish reference agreement or validate an analytical method."@en .
  ex:scene-vr35 cd:narration "RSD becomes unstable when the mean approaches zero and is not meaningful when zero does not represent a true ratio-scale origin. A large ratio may reflect a tiny denominator rather than a large absolute SD. Always inspect the underlying mean, SD, units and measurement scale before interpreting the percentage. A single RSD value cannot communicate all aspects of analytical quality."@en .
  ex:scene-vr36 cd:narration "Replace only QC A's 10.2 mg/L result by 15.2 mg/L while retaining the other four values. The preserved table and plot show that the mean, sample variance and SD all increase. The modification illustrates sensitivity of these summaries to an extreme measurement; it is not a new observed QC dataset. Investigate the cause before considering exclusion, because a large influence on a summary is not a justification for automatic deletion."@en .
  ex:scene-vr37 cd:narration "The QC R exercise compares means, SDs, RSD percentages and counts within the inclusive interval from 9.5 to 10.5 mg/L. The comparisons create logical vectors, and `sum()` counts their `TRUE` entries. Read each pair of outputs in A/B order when running the code in the presentation.\n\nThe interval is illustrative, not a validated requirement, and the five observations per method do not establish long-term performance or trueness."@en .
  ex:scene-se-return cd:narration "Earlier, $\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\sigma^2/n$ explained the difference between the expected MSD and the population variance when the same sample was used to fit the mean.\n\nThe same term has a second interpretation: it describes how sample means vary around $\\mu$ across repeated samples. It therefore provides information about random uncertainty of the estimated mean. It does not reveal the actual error of the one realized sample mean."@en .
  ex:scene-se-sem cd:narration "To make the estimator notation distinct, now call the same measurement variable $Y$ and its sample mean $\\bar Y$. This is a local renaming, not a change in the observations or sampling model.\n\nThe SD $\\sigma$ describes individual measurements; the standard error of $\\bar Y$ is the square root of its sampling variance, $\\sigma/\\sqrt n$. When $\\sigma$ is unknown, $s/\\sqrt n$ estimates that SE.\n\nVariance can quantify estimator uncertainty as well as descriptive spread, but this particular formula is not universal for arbitrary model parameters."@en .
  ex:scene-se-rate cd:narration "At fixed population SD and under the stated independent common finite-variance sampling assumptions, the standard error decreases in proportion to $1/\\sqrt n$. Four times as many independent observations halve the SE, and the theoretical SE tends to zero as $n\\to\\infty$.\n\nIndividual measurement spread does not shrink. Correlated readings and systematic effects may leave uncertainty that averaging does not remove, so this limiting result does not imply vanishing total measurement uncertainty."@en .
  ex:scene-se-example cd:narration "For the five QC A measurements, the observed mean is 10.00 mg/L, the sample variance is $0.025\\,(\\mathrm{mg/L})^2$, and the SD is approximately 0.1581 mg/L. Assuming iid repeats, the estimated SE of $\\bar Y$ is 0.0707 mg/L.\n\nA rounded report is therefore $(10.00\\pm0.07)\\,\\mathrm{mg/L}$, explicitly mean plus or minus one estimated SE with $n=5$. Error bars must state what they represent: this is neither an SD bar nor a 95% confidence interval or a complete uncertainty budget."@en .
  ex:scene-vr39 cd:narration "A shared mean can conceal different observed spreads. MSD describes the sample around its fitted center, while the corrected sample variance estimates population variance under explicit assumptions. SD returns to the original unit, and RSD describes relative spread only on a suitable scale. Precision requires stated conditions and cannot establish trueness. Finally, distinguish SD of individual results from the standard error of an estimated mean, and always state what a reported plus-or-minus quantity represents."@en .
}
